<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Korea</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Korean</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Korea is the peninsula in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, home to a succession of Korean kingdoms. It was deeply connected to Chinese dynasties, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> establishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> there, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> intervening in its wars, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a> defending it against Japanese invasion in the 1590s. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> annexed Korea in 1910, after Qing influence there ended.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/korea.jpg" alt="Korea" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gringer · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korea_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Baekje arose in the Han River basin, around modern Seoul, among the walled-town communities of the Mahan confederacy, which Chinese sources of the 3rd century list as some fifty small polities in south-western <b>Korea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/baekje">Baekje · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Balhae, founded by Goguryeo and Mohe refugees in 698, ruled Manchuria and northern <b>Korea</b> for over two centuries, built Tang-style institutions and capitals such as Sanggyeong, linked Japan with the continent through its embassies, and remains central to Korean and Chinese debates about the history of the north-east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/balhae">Balhae · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Australia fought in <b>Korea</b>, Malaya and Vietnam, where national servicemen served from 1966, led the international force in East Timor in 1999 and joined the wars in Afghanistan and Iraq.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The colonies were assigned ranked roles in the empire's economy: Taiwan supplied sugar and rice; <b>Korea</b> provided rice, labour, and later industrial output; Manchukuo after 1932 supplied coal, iron, and strategic minerals essential for military production.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Goguryeo itself was often called Goryeo from the 5th century, and through the later kingdom of Goryeo that name became the English word <b>Korea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Foreign trade passed through the port of Byeongnando near Kaegyong, visited by Song Chinese, Japanese, Khitan and Jurchen traders and, in the eleventh century, by Arab merchants, through whom the name Goryeo probably travelled west to become <b>Korea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
